title: "Python"
description: "Run QQL fully in-process from Python with the pyqql-edge package."
sidebar:
  order: 4
---

`pyqql-edge` is the edge-enabled Python package: the QQL parser, planner,
runtime, in-process HNSW storage, and optional local ONNX embeddings all live
in the wheel. No Qdrant server, no network at inference time.

{% techBadge name="pyqql-edge" variant="orange" /%}

## Install

Python 3.8+. Wheels are published for Linux x86-64, macOS arm64 (Apple
Silicon), and Windows x86-64 — not macOS Intel (ONNX Runtime publishes no
artifact for it; see [models](/docs/edge/models/)).

{% terminal title="pip" %}
pip install pyqql-edge
{% /terminal %}

## Quick start

```python
import pyqql_edge

client = pyqql_edge.local_executor(
    "./qdrant_data",
    model="bge-small-en-v1.5",
)

client.execute("CREATE COLLECTION docs HYBRID")
client.execute('UPSERT INTO docs VALUES {id: 1, text: "hello from edge"}')

report = client.execute("QUERY 'hello' FROM docs USING dense LIMIT 10")
print(report["results"][0]["data"])

client.close()  # flush before deleting ./qdrant_data
```

{% aside type="note" title="Call close() before deleting the data directory" %}
`close()` flushes the WAL and segments. Deleting `./qdrant_data` while shards
are still open can lose or corrupt data — see
[persistence](/docs/edge/persistence/).
{% /aside %}

## Constructors

### `local_executor()`

Fully offline FastEmbed-backed executor signature:

```python
pyqql_edge.local_executor(
    data_dir,
    on_disk_payload=True,
    *,
    model=None,
    sparse_model=None,
    multi_model=None,
    image_model=None,
    reranker_model=None,
    cache_dir=None,
    show_download_progress=False,
)
```

| Parameter | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `data_dir` | *(required)* | Storage directory path |
| `on_disk_payload` | `True` | Store payloads on disk instead of memory |
| `model` | `None` *(BGESmallENV15)* | Dense ONNX model name, HF code, or alias (`"bge-small-en-v1.5"`) |
| `sparse_model` | `None` | Offline sparse model (e.g. `"splade"`) |
| `multi_model` | `None` | Offline multivector model (e.g. `"bge-m3"`) |
| `image_model` | `None` | Offline CLIP vision model (e.g. `"clip-vision"`) |
| `reranker_model` | `None` | Offline cross-encoder model (e.g. `"bge-reranker-base"`) |
| `cache_dir` | `None` | Directory for downloaded models |
| `show_download_progress` | `False` | Show Hugging Face progress bars |

Usage example:

```python
# Dense + sparse + cross-encoder, payloads kept in memory
client = pyqql_edge.local_executor(
    "./qdrant_data",
    on_disk_payload=False,
    model="bge-small-en-v1.5",
    sparse_model="splade",
    reranker_model="bge-reranker-base",
)
```

Models are locked at construction. Passing a `USING MODEL '…'` clause that
names a different model fails loudly instead of switching silently — see
[models](/docs/edge/models/).

### `http_executor()`

Local storage and search, remote OpenAI-compatible embeddings signature:

```python
pyqql_edge.http_executor(
    data_dir,
    url,
    embed_key,
    embed_model,
    embed_dim,
    on_disk_payload=True,
)
```

Usage example:

```python
client = pyqql_edge.http_executor(
    "./qdrant_data",
    "http://localhost:11434/v1/embeddings",
    "",
    "nomic-embed-text",
    768,
)
```

## Client and Stmt

`local_executor` and `http_executor` return a `Client`:

| Method | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `client.execute(query, *, params=None, on_error="stop")` | Execute a query string, `Stmt`, or a list of either. `on_error` is `"stop"` or `"continue"`; `params` binds `:name` / `?` placeholders |
| `client.execute_hits(query, *, params=None, on_error="stop")` | Typed `ScoredPoint` hits directly |
| `client.execute_async(query, *, params=None, on_error="stop")` | Same, but returns an awaitable that runs on the Tokio runtime without blocking the GIL |
| `client.execute_async_hits(query, *, params=None, on_error="stop")` | Async typed hits |
| `client.explain(query)` | Human-readable plan |
| `client.close()` | Flush and release edge storage. Idempotent |
| `with client:` | Context manager; `close()` runs on `__exit__` |

`execute` accepts a string, a pre-parsed `Stmt`, or a list of either. Arrays
and semicolon-delimited strings are auto-batched. For a multi-statement batch,
pass `params` as a list with one entry per statement — the length must match
the statement count, and each entry is a dict (named) or a list of scalars
(positional). Nested dicts expand to dotted keys (`{"loc": {"lat": 1.0}}`
binds `:loc.lat`).

`Stmt` objects expose `stmt.bind(params=None)` (a new bound `Stmt`),
`stmt.compile_route(params=None)`, and `stmt.to_dict()` / `stmt.to_json()`.

The module also exposes parser helpers with the same names as `pyqql`:
`parse`, `parse_json`, `is_valid` (parse + plan), `tokenize`, `inject_filter`,
`compile_query(query, params=None)`, `bind(query, params=None, *,
truncate_vectors=False)` (accepts a string or a `Stmt`; `Stmt` +
`truncate_vectors=True` returns the readable string), and `explain`.
`inject_filter` works on a `Stmt` or a query string and is the supported way
to scope a client to a tenant. Edge has no custom `SHARD` routing, so a
`SHARD '…'` clause fails at execution with `QQL-EDGE-UNSUPPORTED-SHARD` — see
[capabilities](/docs/edge/capabilities/).

## One-shot execution

Prefer a long-lived `Client` when you run more than one query — it keeps the
ONNX model and edge shards open. For one-off calls, module-level `execute`
and `execute_async` build a temporary client, run, and close:

```python
report = pyqql_edge.execute(
    "QUERY 'hello' FROM docs USING dense LIMIT 5",
    data_dir="./qdrant_data",
    model="bge-small-en-v1.5",
)
```

## Return shape and errors

`execute` returns the canonical report:

```json
{
  "ok": true,
  "results": [
    {
      "ok": true,
      "operation": "QUERY",
      "message": "Found 10 hits",
      "data": {"result": {"points": [{"id": 1, "score": 0.75, "payload": {"text": "hello"}}]}}
    }
  ],
  "succeeded": 1,
  "failed": 0
}
```

Errors are raised as `RuntimeError` (execution) or `SyntaxError` (parsing)
with structured attributes attached: `err.code` (e.g.
`QQL-EDGE-UNSUPPORTED-GROUP-BY`), `err.kind`, and `err.span` (a
`{"start", "end"}` dict when the parser has a span). Offline-unsupported
features always raise — never a silent no-op.

Like `pyqql`, edge `execute` returns an `ExecutionReport` dict subclass with
typed accessors: `report.hits(stmt)` / `report.points(stmt)` return
`ScoredPoint` objects (`id`, `score`, `payload`, `text`, `collection`),
`report.facet(stmt)` returns the normalized facet hits, and `report.count(stmt)`
returns an integer.

## Listing models

```python
models = pyqql_edge.list_embedding_models()
for m in models:
    print(m["name"], m["dim"], m["description"])
```

See [models](/docs/edge/models/) for the model slots and resolution rules.
